From a086729a7bcc97ef7ec19b8f1de258048585d79e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pieroviano <piero.viano@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 25 Jul 2026 06:57:42 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Shaped controls, colour-keyed transparency, exact
 curved-region combination

Closes what EMULATION-GAPS listed as the last non-behavioural gaps.

Control.Region was recorded as absent; it was worse than that - an inert
auto-property. Assigning a region compiled, ran, stored the region and shaped
nothing, so a round button or a skinned splash form silently stayed rectangular.
It now drives GTK's shape-combine: the region's bands become the pixel rectangles
gtk_widget_shape_combine_region takes, a region assigned before the handle
existed is applied at realize (before Load, so user code sees an already-shaped
control), and null clears back to the full rectangle. Substrate limit recorded:
a shape lives on a GDK window and lightweight widgets - buttons, labels - own
none, so on those the region is stored and reported but changes nothing, logged
rather than thrown. Forms and containers do own one and shape for real.

Form.TransparencyKey and AllowTransparency did not exist at all, so the standard
shaped-window idiom (BackColor = X; TransparencyKey = X) did not compile. What is
emulated is that idiom: the window gets an RGBA visual and the background painter
punches the background out with zero alpha under the SOURCE operator when the key
names the form's own BackColor. Per-pixel keying of arbitrary content and
click-through on keyed areas are not emulated - both need an input shape
recomputed from rendered pixels every paint - and IsTransparencySupported reports
whether the screen composites at all, so a form that stays opaque on an X11
session with no compositor is diagnosable.

Curved regions now combine exactly. A combine works on band lists and a path only
ever contributed its bounding rectangle to one, so region.Intersect(circle)
intersected with the circle's square and the corners counted as inside. The
interior of a curve is now obtained the way GDI+ obtains it: filled into an A8
coverage bitmap through the existing path replay, runs of covered pixels become
bands, identical adjacent scanlines merge into one taller band. Paths made only of
rectangles are detected and skip the rasterizer entirely, keeping their exact
floating-point edges; past 16 megapixels the bounding rectangle is still used.

Covered by ControlShapeAndTransparencyFunctionalTests (Gdk.Window.IsShaped is the
oracle for the shape - no managed state reports it) and
RegionCurvedCombineFunctionalTests (a point inside the bounding box and outside
the shape is the oracle for every combine operator). One test from patch 0110 that
pinned the bounding-box fallback now pins the exact result instead.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
